¶
'''abegge†''', ''v. t.'' An old form of ''aby''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|<poem>There dorste no wight hond upon him legge,
That he ne swore he shuld anon ''abegge''.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Reeve’s Tale, l. 18.}}}}

'''abeigh''' (a̤-bēċh′), ''adv.'' [A variant of ME. ''abey'', ''abai'', etc.: see ''bay''<sup>5</sup>, ''n.''] Aloof; at a shy distance. [Scotch.]—'''To stand abeigh''', to keep aloof.
{{smaller block|<poem>Maggie coost her head fu' high,
Look’d asklent an' unco skeigh,
Gart poor Duncan ''stand abeigh''—
Ha, ha, the wooing o’t.</poem>
{{right|''Burns'', Duncan Gray.}}}}

'''abele''' (a-bēl′), ''n.'' [Formerly ''abeele'', ''abeal'', etc., < D. ''abeel'', in comp. ''abeel-boom'', < OF. ''abel'', earlier ''aubel'', < ML. ''albellus'', applied to the white poplar, prop. dim. of L. ''albus'', white.] The white poplar, ''Populus alba'': so called from the white color of its twigs and leaves. See ''poplar''. Also called ''abel-tree'', and sometimes ''abbey''.
{{smaller block|Six ''abeles'' i' the kirkyard grow, on the north side in a row.
{{right|''Mrs. Browning'', Duchess May.}}}}

'''Abelian<sup>1</sup>''' (ā-bel′i-a̤n), ''n.'' [< ''Abel'' + ''-ian''; also ''Abelite'', < LL. ''Abelitæ'', pl., < ''Abel'': see ''-ite''<sup>1</sup>.] A member of a religious sect which arose in northern Africa in the fourth century. {{smaller|The Abelians married, but lived in continence, after the manner, as they maintained, of Abel, and attempted to keep up the sect by adopting the children of others. They are known only from the report of St. Augustine, written after they had become extinct. Also called ''Abelite'' and ''Abelonian''.}}

